    "body": "Your electric vehicle battery may last longer than you think. Ford says its EV batteries retain greater than 90% health on average after 100,000 miles. Here are a few tips to keep yours healthy.\nThe Mustang Mach-E went on sale in late 2020 as Ford’s first fully electric crossover SUV designed from the ground up.\nSince launching the 2021 Mustang Mach-E, Ford has been collecting data from over 36,000 vehicles that have traveled two billion miles across the US and Canada.\nAccording to Ford, the Mach-E’s battery has retained 92% of its health on average over the past five years.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nAcross its full EV lineup, including the F-150 Lightning and E-Transit, Ford said its battery packs maintain more than 90% health on average after 100,000 miles. The key number to look at is the spread, with 95% of vehicles falling between 87% and 97% health.\nOf 2021 model-year EV owners, Ford said, more than 2,000 have crossed 100,000 miles with their original battery averaging better than 91% health, while over 30 have crossed the 200,000-mile mark and average 88% health.\nFord is using real-world data to help owners take good care of their electric vehicles, no matter how much they know about batteries and other EV tech.\nIt’s also applying lessons from developing the Universal Electric Vehicle (UEV) platform, which will underpin its next-generation EVs, starting with the Fantom pickup next year.\nHow to maintain EV battery health over the long term\nFord offered a few simple, everyday tips to help owners support long-term battery health. For one, if possible, charge at home on Level 2 for daily commuting or driving and save DC fast charging for longer trips.\nIt also helps to avoid charging in extreme temperatures if you can. Preconditioning the battery can help bring it into the optimal temperature range, while parking in the shade on hot summer days can also help avoid long-term battery degradation.\nLast, but not least, you can use the Ford App to find charge-level recommendations designed specifically for your vehicle and battery system.\nThe 2021 Mustang Mach-E uses an NCM battery, but some newer Ford batteries use a lithium iron phosphate (LFP) battery chemistry, which is recommended to charge to 100% from time to time.\nFord Mustang Mach-E (left) and F-150 Lightning (right) (Source: Ford)\nFord has introduced new features in the Ford App to help owners maintain battery health specific to their vehicle and will continue to use the data to improve the experience as it introduces its next-gen EVs.\nTop comment by Chris M\nLiked by 4 people\nImportant to recognize that assessing long-term battery health is like looking into outer space, in that the info you're receiving is already out of date. Battery management systems are improving all the time, and more durable chemistries are being released.\nIt will only get better.\nView all comments\nFord will open pre-orders for the Fathom , a midsize electric pickup and first UEV-based model, starting at $28,350 in early 2027.\nMeanwhile, the 2027 Ford Mustang Mach-E is now on sale, starting at $39,995. The 2027 Mach-E drops the standard-range battery offered in the 2026 model and now comes standard with the larger 88 kWh battery with an EPA-estimated range of up to 320 miles (RWD).\nWant to take a closer look at one for yourself? You can find available Ford Mustang Mach-Es around you and see local offers here using our partner link.\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
